        "description": "Final approach targeting."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 206,
        "type": "arrival",
        "label": "Mars approach + Beagle 2 release",
        "description": "2003-12-19. Beagle 2 lander separated for independent EDL toward Isidis Planitia. Mars Express continued to MOI alone."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 206.01,
        "type": "edl_or_oi",
        "label": "Mars orbit insertion",
        "description": "2003-12-25 — Christmas Day. 37-minute main engine burn captured Mars Express into an initial ~250 × 150,000 km orbit, later trimmed to the operational ~298 × 10,107 km near-polar science orbit. Beagle 2 had separated 6 days earlier and landed the same day (2003-12-25) but failed to fully deploy its solar panels — only discovered in 2015 from MRO HiRISE imagery. Mars Express's MARSIS radar later mapped subsurface ice and in 2018 found a bright radar reflector under the south polar cap, read as a subglacial lake — an interpretation still disputed."
      }
